WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.720
Long-lived bats combine strong immune defenses with unusual damaged cell clearance that may guide human aging research. This is Planetarian Daily, episode 181. Bats maintain powerful immune responses while using distinctive mechanisms to clear damaged cells. These traits appear to limit cancer and other age-related conditions over decades. Researchers see the genetic patterns as potential templates for protecting human health during aging.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:58.700
The animals achieve this balance without the elevated metabolic demands observed in other long-lived mammals. The study highlights immune and cellular clearance strategies that operate in tandem across extended lifespans. The BAT study identifies two coordinated traits of robust immunity and efficient removal of damaged cells that together appear to suppress cancer and chronic disease over long lifespans. These strategies operate without the high metabolic costs seen in mammals.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:14.600
Many other long-lived mammals. Understanding how bats maintain both systems could inform therapies that preserve tissue integrity during human aging. One open question is whether similar clearance pathways can be safely enhanced in people without increasing inflammation.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:22.620
The genetic basis for these dual protections may also explain why bats tolerate viruses that cause severe disease and other species.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:39.280
Now shifting to plant roots. When nutrients are limited, roots adjust internal structure, alter metabolism, and recruit beneficial microbes to sustain growth. The mechanisms were identified through combined anatomical, metabolic, and microbiome analyses.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:02:01.520
Cortical cells can form larger air spaces to reduce energy expenditure while changing which bacterial species colonize the root surface. These coordinated responses occur simultaneously rather than sequentially. The findings point to new roots for improving crop resilience. Most people assume roots as fixed pipes that simply grow toward water and nutrients.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:22.040
In reality, roots constantly remodel their internal architecture, metabolism, and microbial partnerships when soil conditions change. Right now, as a plant sits in dry or nutrient poor ground, its root cortical cells may be expanding air spaces to cut energy use while shifting which bacteria it hosts near the root surface.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:29.040
One memorable detail is that some species can reduce root metabolic cost by more than half through these adjustments.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:48.220
These adjustments happen within hours of a stress signal reaching the root tip, allowing rapid matching of root structure to available resources. On a different note, a mouse study maps how acute stress reaches the gastrointestinal tract via the vagus nerve and induces dysmotility that suppresses food intake.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:03:08.080
The pathway links hypothalamic stress centers directly to gut motor control. The work was published in Neuron. The connection explains rapid changes in eating behavior during stress without requiring long-term hormonal shifts. Researchers used targeted neural tracing to confirm the direct route from brain to gut.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:26.320
Next up, a nature study demonstrates how artificial intelligence paired with laboratory methods identifies previously uncharacterized proteins and determines their functions. The approach reveals proteins that standard genomic scans had overlooked. The findings expand the known human proteome.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:36.940
The combination of computational prediction and direct experimental validation allowed researchers to assign roles to sequences previously labeled as non-coding or inactive.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:55.860
This expands the functional map of the human genome beyond earlier annotations. Researchers screened millions of bacteria from landfill soil, zoo environments, larvae guts, and a compost heap near arhus. They isolated 12 enzymes capable of degrading polyurethane foam and nylon.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:07.880
The work came from Arhus University and the Danish Technological Institute. Samples originated from a Kenyan landfill, Rander's Rengskav Tropical Zoo, and local compost material.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:19.280
The enzymes target bonds that conventional recycling methods struggle to break. Teams reconstructed 10 paleogenomes representing the common ancestors of 84 crop species.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:33.000
The sequences highlight genes retained across evolutionary time that confer tolerance to drought and other stresses. The study was led by NRAE, the University of Claremont-Overn, and the CNRS.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:39.900
These shared genes appear in both wheat and barley lineages, despite their divergence millions of years ago.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:53.560
The reconstruction provides a reference for identifying traits that persisted through changing climates. The THINK pen was tested with more than 700 Italian school children, performing standardized writing tasks.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:05:01.520
Data on pressure and motion patterns help identify dysgraphia and dysorthography sooner than conventional assessment alone.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:11.140
The study appeared in PLOS Digital Health. The pen records real-time force and trajectory metrics during BVSC-03 tasks.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:17.060
Early detection allows intervention before handwriting problems become entrenched in later schooling.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:24.400
Atmospheric transport carries plastic fibers and fragments from distant sources into Swiss soil and water bodies.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:32.980
The annual load was quantified through field sampling and modeling. The study documents are previously under-measured environmental pathway.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:43.100
Fibers from clothing and fragments from packaging travel long distances before settling. The total exceeds 200 tons each year across rural areas alone.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:50.220
Isotope analyses link agricultural nutrients and elevated temperatures to coral decline in U.S. waters.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:58.280
Even resilient reef systems now show signs of bleaching and mortality. The study highlights ongoing pressure on these ecosystems.

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:04.320
Nitrogen and phosphorus inputs from fertilizers compound the effects of warmer water temperatures.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:09.620
The combination accelerates tissue loss in species previously considered more tolerant.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:16.320
An international team produced a high-resolution assembly that clarifies the deepest branches of the grass family.

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:23.880
The work includes major food crops and was contributed to by Elizabeth Kellogg at the Donald Danforth Plant Science Center.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:29.580
The new assembly resolves relationships that earlier fragmented genomes left ambiguous.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:38.220
It places key crop lineages in a revised phylogenetic context that alters prior assumptions about grass diversification timing.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:47.000
The robust RNA-aptomer activates dyes through a localized nucleotide rearrangement rather than large structural shifts.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:54.300
The mechanism was detailed by an international group including Ronald Mekura's team at the Institute of Organic Chemistry.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:07:04.140
The finding refines tools for live cell RNA imaging. A small local nucleotide flip within the abdomen is sufficient to turn on fluorescence.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:10.200
This minimal change enables higher resolution tracking of RNA molecules inside living cells.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:17.240
Roughly 80% of U.S. adults report taking dietary supplements or vitamins. The highest share recorded.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:23.160
Many products lack proven efficacy and carry potential risks according to the survey analysis.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:28.180
The New York Times Wells section reported the findings from national survey data.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:34.040
Usage has risen steadily even as evidence for most common supplements remains weak or absent.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.580
The pattern holds across age groups and income levels.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:46.040
Two pediatric deaths linked to viral vector toxicity in Chinese genome editing cases were reported only after a year's delay.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:51.300
The editorial calls for greater transparency in early human gene editing studies.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:55.580
The cases involved viral vectors used in experimental treatments.

00:07:56.140 --> 00:08:00.820
Delayed reporting prevented timely assessment of vector safety across research programs.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:05.680
The Lancet piece urges standardized disclosure timelines for adverse events.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:14.920
Systematic measurements show that the molecular arrangement of water at the catalyst surface directly influences reaction rates under hydrogen evolving conditions.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:19.560
The study addresses a key bottleneck in solar to hydrogen conversion.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:25.500
Interfacial water layers were examined under operating conditions rather than in static models.

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:31.340
Changes in water orientation and hydrogen bonding correlate with measured photo current differences.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.280
The work clarifies why some TIO2 preparations outperform others despite identical bulk composition.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:49.540
Researchers at the Institute of Microbiology of the Chinese Academy of Sciences characterize the BA.3.2.2 branch after it increased in European science.

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:55.880
Structural and functional assays revealed features that differentiated from earlier lineages.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.540
The sub-lineage had remained at low detection levels before rising in prevalence.

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:06.480
The work provides a baseline for monitoring changes in receptor binding and immune escape properties.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:16.340
Before we go, keep an eye on structural assays of the new SARS-CoV-2 sub-lineage RE.2.2 for changes in receptor binding.

